    "body": "U.S. rail traffic increased 7.8% in the week ended Oct. 24 compared with the same week in 2025, as growth in intermodal shipments outpaced gains in carloads , Association of American Railroads data shows.\nWeekly intermodal volume, which includes containers and trailers, rose 11.3%, while carload traffic increased 3.6%. Both measures grew faster than their respective year-to-date rates.\nEight of the 10 carload commodity groups recorded year-over-year increases. Petroleum and petroleum products led percentage growth, rising 15.4%, followed by motor vehicles and parts at 15.3%. Forest products increased 11.7%, and metallic ores and metals gained 11.6%.\nFarm products excluding grain, and food fell 3.7%, while grain posted a rare decline at 1.4%.\nFor the first 42 weeks of 2026, combined U.S. rail traffic increased 3.8% from the same period in 2025. Carloads rose 2.7%, while intermodal traffic was better by 4.7%.\nDespite its weekly decline, grain remained the strongest-growing carload commodity group for the year to date, up 10.6%. Metallic ores and metals followed with an 8.6% increase, while petroleum and petroleum products rose 8%. Coal fell by 1.6%, and motor vehicles and parts were unchanged.\nCombined traffic on the nine reporting U.S., Canadian and Mexican railroads increased 8% for the week. North American carloads rose 5%, while intermodal volume increased 10.8%, according to the AAR.\nCanadian railroads reported weekly carload growth of 7.6% and an intermodal increase of 5.5%. Mexican railroads posted a 12.7% increase in carloads and a 33.4% jump in intermodal traffic.\nFor the first 42 weeks of the year, total North American rail traffic rose 3.3%. Cumulative traffic increased 1.5% in Canada and 5.2% in Mexico.\nWhy it matters: Weekly traffic growth continues to outpace the year-to-date rate, led by intermodal shipments.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\n9 alleged Mexican Cartel associates arrested in California train theft ring\nCPKC reports record revenue on strength of grain and intermodal traffic\nFirst look: CPKC earnings\nBacklog grows as 35,000 containers await rail service at LA–Long Beach\nWabtec earnings rise on higher freight and transit sales",
